  }}
  .footer {{
    text-align: center;
    padding: 32px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }}
  .highlight {{
    background: #E8F5E9;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--success);
  }}
  .warning-highlight {{
    background: #FFF3E0;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--accent);
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Chocolate Retail Sales Performance Report</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">Comprehensive analysis of sales transactions across stores, products, and customer segments</div>
    <div class="meta">Reporting Period: January 2023 – December 2024 &nbsp;|&nbsp; Audience: Retail Management</div>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="container">

<!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<div class="section" id="executive-summary">
  <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
  <p style="font-size:1.05rem; color:#5D4037;">
    Over the 2023–2024 period, the chocolate retail business generated <strong>{fmt_currency(kpi['total_revenue'])}</strong> in total revenue with <strong>{fmt_currency(kpi['total_profit'])}</strong> in profit, maintaining a healthy average profit margin of <strong>{fmt_pct(kpi['avg_profit_margin'])}</strong>. The business showed modest year-over-year growth across all key metrics, with order volume, revenue, and profit all increasing between 1.5% and 1.7%.
  </p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Total Revenue</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt_currency(kpi['total_revenue'])}</div>
      <div class="delta pos">↑ {yoy['revenue_growth']:.2f}% YoY</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Total Profit</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt_currency(kpi['total_profit'])}</div>
      <div class="delta pos">↑ {yoy['profit_growth']:.2f}% YoY</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Avg Profit Margin</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt_pct(kpi['avg_profit_margin'])}</div>
      <div class="delta">Stable across periods</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Avg Order Value</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt_currency(kpi['avg_order_value'])}</div>
      <div class="delta">Consistent</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Total Orders</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt_number(kpi['total_orders'])}</div>
      <div class="delta pos">↑ {yoy['orders_growth']:.2f}% YoY</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="label">Discount Utilization</div>
      <div class="value">{fmt_pct(kpi['discount_utilization'])}</div>
      <div class="delta">of all transactions</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong> While the business maintains strong profitability with a 40% average margin, growth has plateaued at ~1.7% YoY. With 37.6% of transactions using discounts, there is opportunity to optimize promotional strategy to protect margins while driving volume.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SALES TRENDS -->
<div class="section" id="sales-trends">
  <h2>Sales Trends Over Time</h2>
  <p>
    Monthly revenue remained relatively stable throughout the two-year period, fluctuating between approximately $48K and $56K per month. The strongest month was <strong>June 2024</strong> ($55,980), while the weakest was <strong>February 2023</strong> ($48,259). There is no pronounced seasonal peak, though Q3 tends to be slightly stronger than other quarters.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['monthly_trends']}
  </div>

  <h3>Quarterly Performance</h3>
  <p>
    Quarterly revenue shows a gradual upward trend from 2023 to 2024. Q2 2024 was the strongest quarter at $163,384, while Q2 2023 was the weakest at $155,061. The year-over-year quarterly growth ranged from -0.5% (Q4) to +5.4% (Q2).
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['quarterly_comparison']}
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['seasonality']}
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Seasonality Insight:</strong> January and July show the highest combined revenue across both years, suggesting post-holiday restocking and mid-year purchasing patterns. February consistently underperforms, presenting an opportunity for targeted promotional campaigns.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- PRODUCT PERFORMANCE -->
<div class="section" id="product-performance">
  <h2>Product & Category Performance</h2>
  <p>
    The product portfolio comprises <strong>{kpi['unique_products']} unique products</strong>. Revenue distribution is relatively even—the top 10 products account for only 5.8% of total revenue, indicating a well-diversified assortment without over-reliance on a few SKUs.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['top_products']}
  </div>

  <h3>Top 10 Products by Revenue</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rank</th>
        <th>Product ID</th>
        <th class="text-right">Revenue</th>
        <th class="text-right">Profit</th>
        <th class="text-right">Orders</th>
        <th class="text-right">Units Sold</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for i, p in enumerate(data['top_products'][:10], 1):
    html += f'''      <tr>
        <td>{i}</td>
        <td>{p['product_id']}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_currency(p['revenue'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_currency(p['profit'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_number(p['orders'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_number(p['quantity'])}</td>
      </tr>
'''

html += f'''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Note on Product Dimensions:</strong> Dimension tables (products.csv, stores.csv, customers.csv, calendar.csv) referenced in the dataset documentation were not available in the provided data file. Analysis by brand, category, cocoa percentage, and geographic attributes could not be performed. The above rankings are based on product and store IDs only.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- STORE ANALYSIS -->
<div class="section" id="store-analysis">
  <h2>Store & Geographic Performance</h2>
  <p>
    The retail network consists of <strong>{kpi['unique_stores']} stores</strong>. Similar to products, revenue is well distributed—the top 10 stores represent 11.0% of total revenue. Store performance varies by approximately 30% between the highest and lowest revenue locations.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['top_stores']}
  </div>

  <h3>Top 10 Stores by Revenue</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Rank</th>
        <th>Store ID</th>
        <th class="text-right">Revenue</th>
        <th class="text-right">Profit</th>
        <th class="text-right">Orders</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for i, s in enumerate(data['top_stores'][:10], 1):
    html += f'''      <tr>
        <td>{i}</td>
        <td>{s['store_id']}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_currency(s['revenue'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_currency(s['profit'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_number(s['orders'])}</td>
      </tr>
'''

html += f'''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Store Insight:</strong> The revenue gap between top-performing and median stores is modest (~15%), suggesting consistent operational standards. However, investigating best practices from top stores (S075, S076, S094) could help lift underperformers.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- DISCOUNT PROFITABILITY -->
<div class="section" id="discount-profitability">
  <h2>Discount Impact & Profitability Analysis</h2>
  <p>
    Discounts are applied in <strong>{fmt_pct(kpi['discount_utilization'])}</strong> of all transactions, with four discount tiers: 0%, 10%, 15%, and 20%. Counter-intuitively, average profit margin remains stable at ~40% across all discount levels, suggesting that cost structure adjusts proportionally or that discounted transactions involve higher-margin products.
  </p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    {charts['discount_impact']}
  </div>

  <h3>Discount Tier Breakdown</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Discount Level</th>
        <th class="text-right">Orders</th>
        <th class="text-right">Revenue</th>
        <th class="text-right">Avg Order Value</th>
        <th class="text-right">Avg Margin</th>
        <th class="text-right">Revenue/Unit</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
'''

for d in data['discount']:
    rev_per_unit = d['revenue'] / d['total_qty']
    html += f'''      <tr>
        <td>{int(d['discount']*100)}%</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_number(d['orders'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_currency(d['revenue'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_currency(d['avg_aov'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_pct(d['avg_margin'])}</td>
        <td class="text-right">{fmt_currency(rev_per_unit)}</td>
      </tr>
'''

html += f'''    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Actionable Insight:</strong> Discounted transactions show lower average order values ($21.44 at 20% off vs $27.15 at full price) but similar unit volumes. The margin stability is encouraging, yet the revenue per unit erosion ($9.04 → $7.15) means discounts must drive meaningful incremental volume to be profitable. Consider A/B testing 10% vs 15% promotions to find the optimal elasticity point.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- CUSTOMER INSIGHTS -->
